BUG: `objToLocalTS` in /luxon/src/impl/util.js mishandles years 0-99. `Date.UTC(y, ...)` maps years 0-99 to 19XX, so the function subtracts 1900 years afterwards — but it does that on the ALREADY-NORMALIZED date, which silently folds a phantom Feb 29 (the intermediate year 19XX/2000 is a leap year while the target year is not) and shifts results by a day. Example: `DateTime.fromISO("0099-12-31").plus({ day: 61 })` should be 0100-03-02 but returns 0100-03-01.

EDIT: in /luxon/src/impl/util.js replace the body of `objToLocalTS` so that the year fix-up re-applies the original year/month/day instead of only changing the year. The new function must be exactly:

export function objToLocalTS(obj) {
  let d = Date.UTC(
    obj.year,
    obj.month - 1,
    obj.day,
    obj.hour,
    obj.minute,
    obj.second,
    obj.millisecond
  );

  // for legacy reasons, years between 0 and 99 are interpreted as 19XX; revert that
  if (obj.year < 100 && obj.year >= 0) {
    d = new Date(d);
    d.setUTCFullYear(obj.year, obj.month - 1, obj.day);
  }
  return +d;
}

(Only the line `d.setUTCFullYear(d.getUTCFullYear() - 1900);` changes, to `d.setUTCFullYear(obj.year, obj.month - 1, obj.day);`. Keep the surrounding comment and formatting.)

